From: Ankit Pipalia <ankit@example.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 05:20:00 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 18/18] power: supply: qcom_smbx: expose writable float voltage

Phoenix is deployed as a permanently powered headless server, so its battery
should behave as a UPS rather than as a cycling energy store.  The only charge
control the driver exposes today is CHARGE_BEHAVIOUR, and inhibit-charge alone
turns out to be fragile on a marginal input path: with about 1 ohm of series
resistance between source and USBIN, inhibiting at a high cell voltage drops
the input into repeated AICL collapse, so USBIN settles near its 50 mA floor and
the battery supplies most of the system load.  Measured at 4.32 V that was
-35 mA average.  Replacing the cable to bring the path to roughly 0.25-0.4 ohm
restored the intended behaviour on the same adapter (+1.5 mA average), so
inhibit-charge works when, and only when, the path has enough headroom for the
charger to hold VSYS above the cell.

Capping the charge voltage is the more robust control.  With
CHARGING_ENABLE_CMD_BIT left set the charger keeps regulating rather than
handing the rail back to the battery, and the cell rests at the programmed float
voltage with taper current near zero - the behaviour laptop firmware implements
for a "stop charging at 80%" setting.  It also removes the hysteresis cycle
entirely: there is no discharge down to a resume threshold, because the cell is
simply never charged above the ceiling.

FLOAT_VOLTAGE_CFG is already programmed once at probe from the device tree
battery description.  Expose it as CONSTANT_CHARGE_VOLTAGE so a policy daemon
can lower it at runtime.  Writes are rejected outside
[FLOAT_VOLTAGE_BASE_UV, voltage_max_design_uv]: userspace may lower the ceiling
but must never raise it above the value the device tree declares safe, and the
selector truncates so the programmed voltage never exceeds the request.

Signed-off-by: Ankit Pipalia <ankit@example.com>
---
--- a/drivers/power/supply/qcom_smbx.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/qcom_smbx.c
@@ -70,6 +70,8 @@
 
 #define FLOAT_VOLTAGE_CFG				0x70
 #define FLOAT_VOLTAGE_SETTING_MASK			GENMASK(7, 0)
+#define FLOAT_VOLTAGE_BASE_UV				3487500
+#define FLOAT_VOLTAGE_STEP_UV				7500
 
 #define SMB2_FG_UPDATE_CFG_2_SEL			0x7D
 #define SMB2_SOC_LT_CHG_RECHARGE_THRESH_SEL_BIT		BIT(2)
@@ -318,6 +320,7 @@
 	POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_CURRENT_NOW,
 	POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_VOLTAGE_NOW,
 	POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_CHARGE_BEHAVIOUR,
+	POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_CONSTANT_CHARGE_VOLTAGE,
 	POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_STATUS,
 	POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_HEALTH,
 	POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_ONLINE,
@@ -989,6 +992,48 @@
 		POWER_SUPPLY_CHARGE_BEHAVIOUR_AUTO :
 		POWER_SUPPLY_CHARGE_BEHAVIOUR_INHIBIT_CHARGE;
 	return 0;
+}
+
+static int smb_get_float_voltage(struct smb_chip *chip, int *val)
+{
+	unsigned int stat;
+	int rc;
+
+	rc = regmap_read(chip->regmap, chip->base + FLOAT_VOLTAGE_CFG, &stat);
+	if (rc < 0)
+		return rc;
+
+	*val = FLOAT_VOLTAGE_BASE_UV +
+		(stat & FLOAT_VOLTAGE_SETTING_MASK) * FLOAT_VOLTAGE_STEP_UV;
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Userspace may lower the charge ceiling to keep the cell off its maximum
+ * resting voltage, but must never raise it above the device tree's declared
+ * design maximum.  The selector truncates, so the programmed voltage is always
+ * less than or equal to the requested one.
+ */
+static int smb_set_float_voltage(struct smb_chip *chip, int uv)
+{
+	unsigned int sel;
+
+	if (!chip->batt_info || chip->batt_info->voltage_max_design_uv <= 0)
+		return -ENODEV;
+
+	if (uv < FLOAT_VOLTAGE_BASE_UV ||
+	    uv > chip->batt_info->voltage_max_design_uv)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	sel = (uv - FLOAT_VOLTAGE_BASE_UV) / FLOAT_VOLTAGE_STEP_UV;
+	if (sel > FLOAT_VOLTAGE_SETTING_MASK)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	dev_info(chip->dev, "float voltage set to %u uV\n",
+		 FLOAT_VOLTAGE_BASE_UV + sel * FLOAT_VOLTAGE_STEP_UV);
+
+	return regmap_update_bits(chip->regmap, chip->base + FLOAT_VOLTAGE_CFG,
+				  FLOAT_VOLTAGE_SETTING_MASK, sel);
 }
 
 static int smb_get_property(struct power_supply *psy,
@@ -1013,6 +1058,8 @@
 					&val->intval);
 	case POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_CHARGE_BEHAVIOUR:
 		return smb_get_charge_behaviour(chip, &val->intval);
+	case POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_CONSTANT_CHARGE_VOLTAGE:
+		return smb_get_float_voltage(chip, &val->intval);
 	case POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_ONLINE:
 		return smb_get_prop_usb_online(chip, &val->intval);
 	case POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_STATUS:
@@ -1048,6 +1095,8 @@
 		default:
 			return -EINVAL;
 		}
+	case POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_CONSTANT_CHARGE_VOLTAGE:
+		return smb_set_float_voltage(chip, val->intval);
 	default:
 		dev_err(chip->dev, "No setter for property: %d\n", psp);
 		return -EINVAL;
@@ -1059,6 +1108,7 @@
 {
 	switch (psp) {
 	case POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_CHARGE_BEHAVIOUR:
+	case POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_CONSTANT_CHARGE_VOLTAGE:
 		return 1;
 	default:
 		return 0;
-- 
